1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,127
A tailor uses inches.

2
00:00:02,127 --> 00:00:04,872
A road sign uses kilometres per hour.

3
00:00:04,872 --> 00:00:07,160
Physics uses metres per second.

4
00:00:07,560 --> 00:00:09,307
Same world, different units.

5
00:00:09,307 --> 00:00:12,186
So we must switch between
them, without mistakes.

6
00:00:12,636 --> 00:00:16,380
Aarav's bicycle meter reads
eighteen kilometres per hour.

7
00:00:16,730 --> 00:00:19,916
His homework asks for the
speed in metres per second.

8
00:00:19,916 --> 00:00:21,318
Is it still eighteen?

9
00:00:21,818 --> 00:00:25,949
By the end, you will convert it in
one line, and never get it backwards.

10
00:00:26,599 --> 00:00:29,255
So what does converting
a unit really mean?

11
00:00:29,605 --> 00:00:31,497
The quantity stays the same.

12
00:00:31,497 --> 00:00:34,738
Only the unit changes, so
the number changes too.

13
00:00:35,138 --> 00:00:36,695
Remember episode one.

14
00:00:36,695 --> 00:00:39,156
n one u one equals n two u two.

15
00:00:39,756 --> 00:00:44,367
To convert, we multiply by a conversion
factor, a fraction exactly equal

16
00:00:44,367 --> 00:00:45,267
to one.

17
00:00:45,493 --> 00:00:48,422
One kilometre over one
thousand metres is one.

18
00:00:48,422 --> 00:00:51,264
The top and the bottom
are the same length.

19
00:00:51,714 --> 00:00:54,905
Multiplying by one never
changes a quantity.

20
00:00:55,555 --> 00:00:58,257
Let's ride with Aarav
on our motion notebook.

21
00:00:58,607 --> 00:01:00,653
The road markers are in metres.

22
00:01:00,653 --> 00:01:02,993
Watch where he is after each second.

23
00:01:03,393 --> 00:01:06,135
After one second, he has gone five metres.

24
00:01:06,135 --> 00:01:08,146
After two seconds, ten metres.

25
00:01:08,546 --> 00:01:11,286
Let's replay the first
second in slow motion.

26
00:01:11,286 --> 00:01:14,017
Start, and one second later, five metres.

27
00:01:14,467 --> 00:01:16,436
Five metres in every second.

28
00:01:16,436 --> 00:01:20,223
So eighteen kilometres per
hour is five metres per second.

29
00:01:20,723 --> 00:01:22,192
Flip the unit dial.

30
00:01:22,192 --> 00:01:24,182
The bicycle does not change.

31
00:01:24,182 --> 00:01:25,753
Only the number does.

32
00:01:25,753 --> 00:01:27,211
Eighteen, or five.

33
00:01:27,611 --> 00:01:30,143
And on the graph, the slope is the speed.

34
00:01:30,143 --> 00:01:32,089
One line, written in two units.

35
00:01:32,739 --> 00:01:36,044
Now let's do it on paper,
one small step at a time.

36
00:01:36,394 --> 00:01:37,294
Step one.

37
00:01:37,246 --> 00:01:39,385
Write the unit as a fraction.

38
00:01:39,385 --> 00:01:41,721
Eighteen kilometres over one hour.

39
00:01:42,471 --> 00:01:43,371
Step two.

40
00:01:43,366 --> 00:01:45,374
Swap kilometres for metres.

41
00:01:45,374 --> 00:01:48,060
One kilometre is one thousand metres.

42
00:01:48,810 --> 00:01:49,774
Step three.

43
00:01:49,774 --> 00:01:54,292
One hour is sixty times sixty,
three thousand six hundred seconds.

44
00:01:55,042 --> 00:01:55,996
Step four.

45
00:01:55,996 --> 00:01:56,896
Simplify.

46
00:01:56,829 --> 00:02:01,501
One thousand over three thousand
six hundred is five over eighteen.

47
00:02:02,251 --> 00:02:05,010
Eighteen times five over eighteen is five.

48
00:02:05,010 --> 00:02:08,127
Five metres per second,
as the bicycle showed.

49
00:02:08,777 --> 00:02:10,922
Now the reasoning, link by link.

50
00:02:11,272 --> 00:02:13,276
A kilometre is a big unit.

51
00:02:13,276 --> 00:02:15,427
In metres, the number grows.

52
00:02:15,427 --> 00:02:16,874
Times one thousand.

53
00:02:17,274 --> 00:02:20,722
But in one second, you cover
much less than in an hour.

54
00:02:20,722 --> 00:02:22,942
Divide by three thousand six hundred.

55
00:02:23,342 --> 00:02:25,385
Together, the number shrinks.

56
00:02:25,385 --> 00:02:29,461
Five over eighteen is the same
as dividing by three point six.

57
00:02:30,061 --> 00:02:31,123
Remember this.

58
00:02:31,123 --> 00:02:35,453
Kilometres per hour to metres per
second, times five over eighteen.

59
00:02:35,453 --> 00:02:37,996
Back again, times eighteen over five.

60
00:02:38,746 --> 00:02:39,786
Side by side.

61
00:02:39,786 --> 00:02:42,551
On the left, eighteen kilometres per hour.

62
00:02:42,551 --> 00:02:45,076
On the right, five metres per second.

63
00:02:45,476 --> 00:02:47,227
Same bicycle, same speed.

64
00:02:47,227 --> 00:02:50,158
The bigger unit always
gets the smaller number.

65
00:02:50,758 --> 00:02:51,819
Now you try.

66
00:02:51,819 --> 00:02:53,918
Seventy-two kilometres per hour.

67
00:02:53,918 --> 00:02:57,675
In metres per second, is the
number bigger or smaller?

68
00:02:57,675 --> 00:02:58,993
Pause and decide.

69
00:03:02,193 --> 00:03:03,093
Smaller!

70
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:07,333
Seventy-two times five over eighteen
is twenty metres per second.

71
00:03:07,983 --> 00:03:09,115
Now a twist.

72
00:03:09,115 --> 00:03:10,576
Units with powers.

73
00:03:10,926 --> 00:03:13,825
One metre is one hundred centimetres.

74
00:03:13,825 --> 00:03:14,725
Easy.

75
00:03:14,835 --> 00:03:17,599
But take a square, one metre on each side.

76
00:03:17,599 --> 00:03:19,504
Its area is one square metre.

77
00:03:19,854 --> 00:03:23,650
In centimetres, each side is
one hundred centimetres long.

78
00:03:23,650 --> 00:03:25,673
So how big is the area now?

79
00:03:26,123 --> 00:03:27,023
Your turn.

80
00:03:27,012 --> 00:03:30,312
How many square centimetres
make one square metre?

81
00:03:30,312 --> 00:03:32,457
One hundred, or ten thousand?

82
00:03:32,457 --> 00:03:33,765
Pause and decide.

83
00:03:36,965 --> 00:03:37,952
Ten thousand!

84
00:03:37,952 --> 00:03:39,938
One hundred times one hundred.

85
00:03:39,938 --> 00:03:43,124
When the unit is squared,
square the factor too.

86
00:03:43,724 --> 00:03:45,413
For a cube, cube it.

87
00:03:45,413 --> 00:03:48,669
One cubic metre is one
million cubic centimetres.

88
00:03:49,319 --> 00:03:51,054
Now, one worked example.

89
00:03:51,404 --> 00:03:55,257
Water has a density of about
one gram per cubic centimetre.

90
00:03:55,257 --> 00:03:57,981
What is it in kilograms per cubic metre?

91
00:03:58,481 --> 00:03:59,381
Step one.

92
00:03:59,350 --> 00:04:02,296
One gram is one thousandth of a kilogram.

93
00:04:02,296 --> 00:04:05,242
That is ten to the minus three kilograms.

94
00:04:05,992 --> 00:04:06,906
Step two.

95
00:04:06,906 --> 00:04:10,354
One centimetre is ten
to the minus two metres.

96
00:04:10,354 --> 00:04:11,254
Cube it.

97
00:04:11,213 --> 00:04:15,464
One cubic centimetre is ten
to the minus six cubic metres.

98
00:04:16,214 --> 00:04:17,114
Step three.

99
00:04:17,079 --> 00:04:17,979
Divide.

100
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:22,131
Ten to the minus three, over ten to
the minus six, is ten to the power

101
00:04:22,131 --> 00:04:23,031
three.

102
00:04:23,455 --> 00:04:28,092
So the density of water is about one
thousand kilograms per cubic metre.

103
00:04:28,742 --> 00:04:30,208
Now, the exam trap.

104
00:04:30,558 --> 00:04:35,027
Many students multiply thirty-six
kilometres per hour by eighteen over

105
00:04:35,027 --> 00:04:38,778
five, and get one hundred
and twenty-nine point six.

106
00:04:39,128 --> 00:04:40,414
That is backwards.

107
00:04:40,414 --> 00:04:42,301
The number must get smaller.

108
00:04:42,301 --> 00:04:46,025
Thirty-six times five over
eighteen is ten metres per second.

109
00:04:46,675 --> 00:04:48,128
Where is this asked?

110
00:04:48,128 --> 00:04:52,680
As the first step of numericals:
speeds, densities, and squared or cubed

111
00:04:52,680 --> 00:04:53,580
units.

112
00:04:53,875 --> 00:04:55,066
Exam level now.

113
00:04:55,066 --> 00:04:58,287
g is nine point eight
metres per second squared.

114
00:04:58,287 --> 00:05:01,144
What is it in kilometres per hour squared?

115
00:05:01,144 --> 00:05:02,385
Pause and solve.

116
00:05:07,785 --> 00:05:09,099
The answer is B.

117
00:05:09,099 --> 00:05:12,409
About one point two seven
times ten to the five.

118
00:05:12,909 --> 00:05:15,805
Metres to kilometres,
divide by a thousand.

119
00:05:15,805 --> 00:05:20,386
Per second squared to per hour squared,
times three thousand six hundred,

120
00:05:20,386 --> 00:05:21,286
squared.

121
00:05:21,590 --> 00:05:23,047
A forgot to square.

122
00:05:23,047 --> 00:05:24,656
C forgot the thousand.

123
00:05:24,656 --> 00:05:26,214
D flipped the factor.

124
00:05:26,814 --> 00:05:28,271
Here is your recap.

125
00:05:28,271 --> 00:05:29,768
Screenshot this card.

126
00:05:32,968 --> 00:05:35,804
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

127
00:05:36,204 --> 00:05:37,104
One.

128
00:05:36,745 --> 00:05:41,259
Why is eighteen kilometres per hour
not eighteen metres per second?

129
00:05:42,159 --> 00:05:43,059
Two.

130
00:05:42,675 --> 00:05:46,522
What is fifty-four kilometres
per hour in metres per second?

131
00:05:47,422 --> 00:05:48,322
Three.

132
00:05:48,036 --> 00:05:51,858
How many cubic centimetres
make one cubic metre, and why?

133
00:05:52,958 --> 00:05:56,474
Next time: can you add five
metres to five seconds?

134
00:05:56,474 --> 00:05:59,668
We find out in dimensions
of physical quantities.
